    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "Choose a suitable unit of area for each surface and give a reason.\n(a) The head of a small insect.\n(b) A basketball court.\n(c) A farm paddock.\n(d) A whole city.",
        "solution_text": "(a) **$\\text{mm}^2$**: the insect’s head is a tiny surface, only a few millimetres across.\n(b) **$\\text{m}^2$**: a basketball court is several metres across, so square metres give a convenient number.\n(c) **ha**: hectares are commonly used for large areas of farmland.\n(d) **$\\text{km}^2$**: a whole city is a large region, so square kilometres give a convenient number."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Two areas are correctly recorded in square metres.\nA coin: $0.0006\\text{ m}^2$.\nA courtyard: $67\\text{ m}^2$.\nFor each, say whether square metres are a suitable choice. Suggest a better unit where needed.",
        "solution_text": "The coin’s area is a very small decimal in square metres. A coin is only a few centimetres across, so **$\\text{cm}^2$** is a more convenient choice.\nThe courtyard is a room-sized outdoor surface. **$\\text{m}^2$** is suitable and gives the convenient number $67$.\nThe same unit need not be the best choice for two surfaces of very different sizes."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Put these units of area in order from smallest to largest:\n$\\text{km}^2$, $\\text{cm}^2$, ha, $\\text{m}^2$, $\\text{mm}^2$.\nExplain how you decided.",
        "solution_text": "The units are squares with sides $1$ mm, $1$ cm, $1$ m, $100$ m and $1000$ m.\nA square with a longer side has a larger area.\n**$\\text{mm}^2$, $\\text{cm}^2$, $\\text{m}^2$, ha, $\\text{km}^2$**."
      }
    ],
